Q: Micrometer Counter backed by DB aggregate causes Prometheus rate() to exceed 100% and spikes on app restart A: <p>There are many issues here I think. The issue you are describing is probably caused by non-atomicity. What I mean by this that probably <code>oidc_session_success_total</code> and <code>oidc_session_total</code> are both querying the data source at different times and if there is an update in the meantime, one of them gets stale the same thing can happen with <code>getTotalSuccess()</code> and <code>oidcSessionSuccessTotal.count()</code> but there is not much code in the question to say it for sure.</p>
<p>How to improve things (only the first two are for your exact issue, the rest are general improvements):</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Instead of using a counter, use a <code>FunctionCounter</code> for async values, query the DB once e.g.: in a scheduled job (also check if update is atomic) and put all the values you need into an immutable state object. Then create <code>FunctionCounter</code>s to make those values available on the Prometheus endpoint to minimize values being stale. Since it seems you also control <code>scrape</code>, you can guard this with a lock so the state object will not be updated during scraping.</p>
</li>
<li><p>As an alternative to a scheduled job, you can update your state object in one of the <code>FunctionCounter</code>s. You can do this by saving the last update time when you update it and check if it was long enough so that if you have two <code>FunctionCounter</code>s only the first one will update the state object, the other one will just use it without updating it. Here is an example with a <code>Gauge</code> but you can replace it to <code>FunctionCounter</code>:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>AtomicLong lastCalculatedTime = new AtomicLong(0);
AtomicLong gaugeValue = new AtomicLong(0);
Gauge.builder(&quot;expensive&quot;, () -&gt; {
    long curr = System.nanoTime();
    return gaugeValue.updateAndGet(old -&gt;
            lastCalculatedTime.updateAndGet(ts -&gt; curr - ts &gt; 10_000 ? curr : ts) == curr ? 0L /* new */ : old);
});
</code></pre>
<p>In this example <code>gaugeValue</code> is the state object but you will have an object here that holds multiple values from the DB (you can use <code>AtomicReference</code>) and <code>0L</code> represents fetching the new value from the DB.</p>
<p>I would also recommend checking the values of the state object when you create it and validate them (to see if DB updates are atomic or not), if the value is invalid, emit a log entry to have a better picture about what is going on and have a better chance to debug and fix it.</p>
<ol start="3">
<li><p>The naming convention in Micrometer is <code>.</code> (dot) instead of <code>_</code>. Micrometer converts <code>.</code> to the naming convention of the backend you use so that you can change backends without changing your code. By using <code>_</code> you break this capability and you will need to change every meter you create if you want to change backends (or use multiple ones at the same time).</p>
</li>
<li><p>Encoding dimension to the name of your meter is bad practice (<code>success</code> in your meter name). Instead of naming it <code>oidc_session_success</code>, you should name both  <code>oidc.sessions</code> and have an &quot;outcome&quot; tag (success/failure/etc) that differentiates the different outcomes so that your time series will look like this in Prometheus:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>oidc_sessions_total{stage=&quot;PROD&quot;, outcome=&quot;success&quot;}
oidc_sessions_total{stage=&quot;PROD&quot;, outcome=&quot;failure&quot;}
</code></pre>
<p>So that you can query the number of success/failure events:</p>
<pre><code>oidc_sessions_total{stage=&quot;PROD&quot;, outcome=&quot;success&quot;}
</code></pre>
<p>and the total events (not specifying the tag will query all the possible values):</p>
<pre><code>sum(oidc_sessions_total{stage=&quot;PROD&quot;})
</code></pre>

Q: Lightning Web Component to display JSON String is not working A: <p>You don't actually need to serialize the wrapper in apex and then parse in the component explicitly, the framework does the job by itself!</p>
<pre><code>public class JSONDemoController {
   @AuraEnabled   //change return type to ResponseJSONWrapper 
   public static ResponseJSONWrapper getContactWithRelatedDataById() {

    String response = '';
    ResponseJSONWrapper wrapper = new ResponseJSONWrapper();
    wrapper.spouse = 'Test Spouse';
    wrapper.mailingAddress = 'Test Address';
    return wrapper;   //return the wrapper itself
}
</code></pre>
<p>and in .js file</p>
<pre><code>findDetails()
  .then(result =&gt; {
      var responseObj = result;
      ...
  })
</code></pre>
<p>This way the code will be less cluttered with not-needed code :)</p>

Q: How to simulate PC&#39;s Esc key on android A: <p>I solved it this way:
I createda a screen keyboard with this onResume callback:</p>
<pre><code>InputConnection conn;

@Override
public void onResume() {
    super.onResume();

    View target_view = getActivity().findViewById( android.R.id.content );
    conn = new BaseInputConnection( target_view, true );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then I created this OnClickListener for all keyboards on the keyboard (only callback is shown):</p>
<pre><code>@Override
public void onClick( View v ) {
    HardKeyEmulatorView vv = (HardKeyEmulatorView) v;

    if( conn != null ) {
        int hard_code = /* compute the Android scan code of the key here */;
        conn.sendKeyEvent( new KeyEvent( KeyEvent.ACTION_DOWN, hard_code ) );
        conn.sendKeyEvent( new KeyEvent( KeyEvent.ACTION_UP, hard_code ) );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that &quot;Esc&quot; keyboard key produces different codes in different Android versions. It produces <code>KeyEvent.KEYCODE_ESCAPE</code> in Android 6 and lower versins but <code>KeyEvent.KEYCODE_BACK</code> in Android 9 and higher versions.</p>
